import React, { useMemo, useState } from 'react';
import { ArrowLeft, ArrowRight, BookOpen, CheckCircle2, Code2, Copy, GraduationCap, Terminal, Search, RotateCcw, ListChecks } from 'lucide-react';
import logo from './assets/logo.png';
import './java-course.css';

const L = (section, title, time, content, points, code, output, walkthrough, challenge) => ({ section, title, time, content, points, code, output, walkthrough, challenge });
const lessons = [
L('01 · Getting started','What is Java?','8 min','Java is a general-purpose, class-based programming language used for backend services, Android-related ecosystems, enterprise software, desktop tools and more. You write source code, compile it to bytecode, and run that bytecode on a compatible Java Virtual Machine.', ['Java is statically typed and strongly associated with object-oriented programming.','The compiler catches many errors before the program runs.','The JVM helps the same bytecode run on different supported operating systems.'], 'public class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        System.out.println("Hello, Java!");\n    }\n}', 'Hello, Java!', 'Line 1 declares a class named Main; a public class is normally stored in Main.java. Line 2 declares the main method, the standard entry point used by the launcher. String[] args receives optional command-line arguments. Line 3 calls println to print text and then move to a new line. The braces mark the class and method bodies.', 'Change the message to print your name and one reason you are learning Java.'),
L('01 · Getting started','Install JDK and run a program','8 min','The JDK (Java Development Kit) contains tools for building Java programs, including javac. Check that a JDK is installed before compiling. In a terminal, move to the folder containing Main.java.', ['Use a current supported JDK version.','Compile source with javac Main.java.','Run the compiled class with java Main; do not add .class.'], 'javac -version\njavac Main.java\njava Main', 'A JDK version is printed, then the program output appears.', 'The first command prints the compiler version and checks that javac is on PATH. The second command compiles Main.java into Main.class bytecode. The third asks the Java launcher to find and execute the Main class. If a command is not found, check the JDK installation and PATH configuration.', 'Run each command separately and write down what each command does.'),
L('01 · Getting started','JDK vs JRE vs JVM','7 min','Think of Java execution as a small production line: the JDK provides development tools, javac converts source code to bytecode, and the JVM executes bytecode. JRE is a traditional name for the runtime components; modern distributions package runtime pieces in different ways.', ['JDK = tools for development plus runtime components.','JVM = engine that executes Java bytecode.','Bytecode is not the same as native machine code.'], 'Main.java  --javac-->  Main.class  --JVM-->  running program', 'Source code → bytecode → program execution', 'You edit Main.java, a human-readable source file. javac checks and compiles it to Main.class. The JVM loads the class, verifies bytecode, and executes it; frequently used code may be optimized by a JIT compiler. The JVM implementation is platform-specific, while the bytecode format is designed to be portable.', 'Explain the flow in your own words without looking at the lesson.'),
L('01 · Getting started','Your first Java program, line by line','10 min','A minimal Java application has a class and a main method. Learn what each keyword and punctuation mark contributes rather than memorising the whole block.', ['Java statements usually end with a semicolon.','Braces group a class or method body.','Java is case-sensitive: Main and main are different names.'], 'public class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        System.out.println("Welcome to 2409 Academy!");\n        System.out.print("Learn Java step by step.");\n    }\n}', 'Welcome to 2409 Academy!\nLearn Java step by step.', 'public makes the class accessible; class introduces a class declaration; Main is its identifier. public static void main(String[] args) is the launch method signature: public allows the launcher to call it, static means no object is required, void means it returns no value, and String[] args stores command-line text. println prints and adds a newline; print does not add one. Quotes delimit string text. Each semicolon ends a statement.', 'Predict the output if the second print call is changed to println, then test it.'),
L('02 · Core syntax','Comments and readable code','5 min','Comments explain intent to people reading the program. The compiler ignores ordinary comments, so use them to clarify why a decision exists rather than narrating every obvious line.', ['// comments continue to the end of the line.','/* ... */ comments can span lines.','Javadoc comments /** ... */ can document APIs.'], 'public class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        // Store the price in rupees.\n        double price = 149.50;\n        /* Apply a student discount\n           in the next version. */\n        System.out.println(price);\n    }\n}', '149.5', 'The single-line comment starts with // and ends at the line break. The double value 149.50 is stored in price. The block comment begins with /* and ends with */ even across two lines. Neither comment is executed; the println statement outputs the value.', 'Add a comment explaining the purpose of one variable in your own program.'),
L('02 · Core syntax','Variables and assignment','10 min','A variable is a named storage location whose declared type determines which values it can hold. Declaration introduces the type and name; assignment stores a value. A variable can be reassigned when its type permits it.', ['Use meaningful camelCase names such as totalMarks.','The = symbol assigns a value; it does not mean mathematical equality.','Local variables must be assigned before they are read.'], 'int age = 20;\nage = 21;\nint nextYearAge = age + 1;\nSystem.out.println(nextYearAge);', '22', 'Line 1 declares an int variable and initialises it to 20. Line 2 replaces the stored value with 21. Line 3 calculates 21 + 1 and stores 22 in a new variable. The last line prints the new value. The semicolons terminate each statement.', 'Create variables for your name, semester number and CGPA; print them.'),
L('02 · Core syntax','Primitive data types','12 min','Java has eight primitive types: byte, short, int, long, float, double, char and boolean. Choose a type based on the range and meaning of the data. String is a reference type, not a primitive.', ['int is a common default for whole numbers.','double is a common default for decimal calculations.','Use L for long literals and f for float literals when needed.'], 'byte level = 2;\nint students = 120;\nlong population = 8000000L;\nfloat temperature = 32.5f;\ndouble cgpa = 8.75;\nchar grade = \'A\';\nboolean passed = true;\nString name = "Balaji";\nSystem.out.println(name + " " + cgpa + " " + passed);', 'Balaji 8.75 true', 'Each declaration states a type, identifier, assignment operator and value. byte stores a small integer; int stores ordinary integers; long handles larger integer values. float and double represent floating-point numbers, with double generally providing more precision. char stores one UTF-16 code unit in single quotes. boolean stores true or false. String stores text in double quotes and is a reference type.', 'Add one variable of each primitive type and print each value.'),
L('02 · Core syntax','Constants with final','5 min','Use final when a variable should be assigned only once. Constants are often named in UPPER_SNAKE_CASE when they are static final class constants.', ['final prevents reassignment after initialisation.','Use constants for values with a clear meaning.','A constant reduces accidental changes and makes code easier to maintain.'], 'public class Main {\n    static final double GST_RATE = 0.18;\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        double price = 1000;\n        double gst = price * GST_RATE;\n        System.out.println(gst);\n    }\n}', '180.0', 'static final double GST_RATE declares a class-level constant shared by the class. price stores 1000. Multiplying price by 0.18 calculates 180.0. Trying to assign a different value to GST_RATE later will produce a compile-time error.', 'Add a final constant for a discount rate and calculate the discounted price.'),
L('02 · Core syntax','Operators and expressions','12 min','Operators combine values into expressions. Arithmetic operators include +, -, *, / and %. Relational operators compare values, logical operators combine boolean conditions, and compound assignments update a variable.', ['Integer division drops the fractional part: 7 / 2 is 3.','% returns the remainder.','Use parentheses to make complex expressions clear.'], 'int a = 7, b = 2;\nSystem.out.println(a + b);\nSystem.out.println(a / b);\nSystem.out.println(a % b);\nSystem.out.println(a > b && b > 0);', '9\n3\n1\ntrue', 'The first println adds 7 and 2. The second performs integer division because both operands are ints, so the fraction is discarded. The remainder operator returns 1. The final expression checks both comparisons; && is true only when both sides are true.', 'Predict the result of 7.0 / 2 and compare it with 7 / 2.'),
L('02 · Core syntax','Type casting and conversion','9 min','Widening conversions move to a type that can represent a broader range, such as int to double. Narrowing conversions, such as double to int, require an explicit cast and can lose information.', ['Widening conversions are generally automatic.','Narrowing casts may truncate or overflow.','Parsing text such as "42" is different from casting a numeric value.'], 'int whole = 9;\ndouble exact = whole;\ndouble price = 19.99;\nint roundedDown = (int) price;\nSystem.out.println(exact);\nSystem.out.println(roundedDown);', '9.0\n19', 'The int value 9 widens to double 9.0 without a cast. The expression (int) price explicitly converts 19.99 to int; it truncates the fractional part rather than rounding, giving 19. Use Math.round when rounding is intended.', 'Try casting -19.99 to int and explain the result.'),
L('03 · Input and output','Printing and formatted output','6 min','System.out.println prints a value followed by a newline. printf formats values using placeholders, which is useful for reports and aligned output.', ['%s formats text, %d integers, and %.2f two decimal places.','printf does not automatically add a newline.','Use \n or %n for a line break.'], 'String name = "Asha";\nint score = 92;\ndouble average = 91.456;\nSystem.out.printf("%s scored %d; average = %.2f%n", name, score, average);', 'Asha scored 92; average = 91.46', 'The format string contains placeholders. %s is replaced by name, %d by score, and %.2f by average rounded to two decimal places for display. %n inserts a platform-appropriate line separator. The comma-separated arguments supply values in placeholder order.', 'Print a product name, quantity and price in one formatted line.'),
L('03 · Input and output','Read input with Scanner','10 min','Scanner is a beginner-friendly way to read keyboard input. It can parse whole numbers, decimal values and tokens. Be careful when mixing nextInt or next with nextLine because the newline can remain in the input buffer.', ['Import java.util.Scanner to use Scanner.','Create Scanner with System.in.','Close the scanner when finished reading console input.'], 'import java.util.Scanner;\n\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Scanner input = new Scanner(System.in);\n        System.out.print("Enter your age: ");\n        int age = input.nextInt();\n        System.out.println("Next year: " + (age + 1));\n        input.close();\n    }\n}', 'Enter your age: 20\nNext year: 21', 'The import makes Scanner available by its short name. new Scanner(System.in) reads from the keyboard. nextInt parses the entered token as an integer. age + 1 calculates the next age, and string concatenation joins it to the label. close releases the scanner resource after input is complete.', 'Read a name and marks, then print a personalised result.'),
L('04 · Decisions','if, else if and else','10 min','Conditional statements choose which block to execute based on a boolean expression. Put the most specific conditions first when ranges overlap.', ['if runs a block when its condition is true.','else if checks another condition only if earlier conditions failed.','else handles all remaining cases.'], 'int marks = 76;\nif (marks >= 90) {\n    System.out.println("A grade");\n} else if (marks >= 60) {\n    System.out.println("B grade");\n} else {\n    System.out.println("Needs practice");\n}', 'B grade', 'marks >= 90 is false, so the first block is skipped. marks >= 60 is true, so B grade is printed and the rest of the chain is skipped. Braces group statements into each branch.', 'Test values 95, 60, 59 and 0. What does each print?'),
L('04 · Decisions','switch statements','8 min','switch selects a branch based on a value. The modern arrow form avoids accidental fall-through and is concise for mapping discrete values to outcomes.', ['Use switch when comparing one expression against discrete cases.','The arrow form is available in modern Java versions.','Handle unexpected values with default.'], 'int day = 6;\nString name = switch (day) {\n    case 1 -> "Monday";\n    case 6 -> "Saturday";\n    case 7 -> "Sunday";\n    default -> "Weekday";\n};\nSystem.out.println(name);', 'Saturday', 'The switch expression evaluates day. When day equals 6, the matching case produces the string Saturday. The result is assigned to name. default supplies a value when no listed case matches.', 'Add cases for Tuesday and Friday, and test an invalid day number.'),
L('04 · Decisions','Boolean logic and truth tables','8 min','Boolean expressions evaluate to true or false. && means AND, || means OR, and ! negates a boolean. Short-circuit operators skip the right side when its result cannot affect the answer.', ['A && B is true only when both are true.','A || B is true when at least one is true.','!A reverses the boolean value.'], 'int age = 20;\nboolean hasId = true;\nboolean canEnter = age >= 18 && hasId;\nSystem.out.println(canEnter);\nSystem.out.println(!hasId);', 'true\nfalse', 'age >= 18 evaluates to true. Because hasId is also true, true && true gives true. !hasId reverses true to false. In real applications, validate each input separately when useful for error messages.', 'Change hasId to false and predict both results.'),
L('05 · Loops','while loop','7 min','A while loop repeats as long as its condition is true. Ensure something inside the loop eventually changes the condition, otherwise the loop may never end.', ['Condition is checked before each iteration.','Initialise the counter before the loop.','Update the counter to avoid an accidental infinite loop.'], 'int count = 1;\nwhile (count <= 3) {\n    System.out.println(count);\n    count++;\n}', '1\n2\n3', 'count begins at 1. The condition count <= 3 is checked. The body prints count and count++ increases it by one. Once count becomes 4, the condition is false and execution continues after the loop.', 'Print the even numbers from 2 through 20.'),
L('05 · Loops','for loop','8 min','A for loop places initialisation, condition and update in one header. It is a natural choice when you know how a counter progresses.', ['Initialisation runs once.','Condition is checked before each iteration.','Update runs after the body on each completed iteration.'], 'for (int i = 1; i <= 5; i++) {\n    System.out.println(i + " squared = " + (i * i));\n}', '1 squared = 1\n2 squared = 4\n3 squared = 9\n4 squared = 16\n5 squared = 25', 'int i = 1 creates the counter. i <= 5 keeps the loop running through 5. i++ increments after each print. The multiplication i * i calculates the square, and + joins numbers with the text.', 'Use a for loop to print a multiplication table for any number.'),
L('05 · Loops','do-while loop','6 min','A do-while loop runs its body at least once because the condition is checked after the body. It is useful for menu prompts that must be shown before validating the response.', ['The body executes at least once.','The semicolon after while(condition) is required.','Use while when the body might need to run zero times.'], 'int attempt = 1;\ndo {\n    System.out.println("Attempt " + attempt);\n    attempt++;\n} while (attempt <= 3);', 'Attempt 1\nAttempt 2\nAttempt 3', 'The do block prints and increments attempt before the condition is evaluated. After attempt reaches 4, attempt <= 3 is false and the loop ends. The trailing semicolon completes the do-while statement.', 'Rewrite the example so it executes exactly once.'),
L('05 · Loops','break, continue and nested loops','9 min','break exits the nearest loop. continue skips the remainder of the current iteration. Nested loops put one loop inside another and are common in grids and multiplication tables.', ['Use break for an intentional early exit.','continue skips to the next iteration.','Nested loops can increase runtime quickly as input grows.'], 'for (int i = 1; i <= 5; i++) {\n    if (i == 3) continue;\n    if (i == 5) break;\n    System.out.println(i);\n}', '1\n2\n4', 'When i is 3, continue jumps to the next iteration before printing. Values 1 and 2 are printed. At i = 4, it prints 4. At i = 5, break exits the loop before the print statement.', 'Print numbers 1 to 10 but skip multiples of 3.'),
L('06 · Methods','Create and call methods','10 min','A method names a reusable operation. Methods help split a large problem into small, testable units. A method declaration specifies access, return type, name and parameters.', ['Call a method by using its name and passing arguments.','void means the method returns no value.','Parameters are variables declared in the method signature.'], 'public class Main {\n    static void greet(String name) {\n        System.out.println("Hello, " + name);\n    }\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        greet("Balaji");\n        greet("Asha");\n    }\n}', 'Hello, Balaji\nHello, Asha', 'greet declares a method that accepts a String parameter called name. It prints a greeting. main calls greet twice with different arguments; each call runs the same reusable method body with a different value.', 'Create a method called showWelcome that prints three lines.'),
L('06 · Methods','Return values and parameters','9 min','A non-void method returns a value using return. The caller can store that result, print it, or use it in another expression. Choose parameter and return types that communicate what the method needs and produces.', ['The returned value must match the declared return type.','Code after a return in the same path is unreachable.','Keep methods focused on one responsibility.'], 'static int add(int a, int b) {\n    return a + b;\n}\n\nint total = add(12, 8);\nSystem.out.println(total);', '20', 'The method add receives two int parameters. return a + b computes and sends their sum back to the caller. add(12, 8) returns 20, which is stored in total and printed.', 'Write a method that returns the larger of two integers.'),
L('06 · Methods','Method overloading','8 min','Overloading means defining methods with the same name but different parameter lists. Java selects a matching overload using the arguments supplied; changing only the return type is not enough.', ['Parameter count, types or order must differ.','Overloads can make an API easier to use.','Avoid confusing overload sets with ambiguous calls.'], 'static int area(int side) { return side * side; }\nstatic int area(int width, int height) { return width * height; }\n\nSystem.out.println(area(4));\nSystem.out.println(area(4, 6));', '16\n24', 'The first area method accepts one int and calculates a square. The second accepts two ints and calculates a rectangle. Java chooses the first overload for area(4) and the second for area(4, 6).', 'Overload a method called greet for a name and for a name plus language.'),
L('07 · Arrays and strings','Arrays fundamentals','10 min','An array stores a fixed number of elements of one type. Indexes start at zero. Use length to learn the number of slots; the final valid index is length - 1.', ['Array length is fixed after creation.','Accessing an invalid index throws ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException.','Use loops to process each element.'], 'int[] marks = {80, 92, 75};\nSystem.out.println(marks[0]);\nSystem.out.println(marks.length);\nmarks[1] = 95;\nSystem.out.println(marks[1]);', '80\n3\n95', 'int[] marks declares an array of integers and initialises three elements. marks[0] reads the first element. length returns 3. marks[1] = 95 replaces the second element, and the last line prints the updated value.', 'Find the sum and average of an integer array using a loop.'),
L('07 · Arrays and strings','Loop through arrays','8 min','A standard for loop gives you the index; an enhanced for loop gives you each element directly. Choose based on whether you need the position or only the value.', ['Use i < array.length, not i <= array.length.','Enhanced for loops are clear when the index is unnecessary.','A running total can calculate a sum in one pass.'], 'int[] numbers = {3, 5, 7};\nint sum = 0;\nfor (int number : numbers) {\n    sum += number;\n}\nSystem.out.println(sum);', '15', 'sum starts at zero. The enhanced for loop assigns each array element to number in turn. sum += number is shorthand for sum = sum + number. After 3 + 5 + 7, sum is 15.', 'Find the maximum element in an array without sorting it.'),
L('07 · Arrays and strings','Two-dimensional arrays','9 min','A two-dimensional array is an array whose elements are themselves arrays. It can represent a table or grid using row and column indexes.', ['Use matrix[row][column] to access an element.','The outer length is the number of rows.','Rows can have different lengths in a jagged array.'], 'int[][] matrix = {\n    {1, 2, 3},\n    {4, 5, 6}\n};\nSystem.out.println(matrix[1][2]);\nfor (int[] row : matrix) {\n    for (int value : row) System.out.print(value + " ");\n    System.out.println();\n}', '6\n1 2 3 \n4 5 6 ', 'matrix[1][2] selects row index 1 (the second row) and column index 2 (the third value), which is 6. The outer loop visits each row array; the inner loop prints each value in that row. println moves to a new line after each row.', 'Calculate the sum of the main diagonal of a square matrix.'),
L('07 · Arrays and strings','String methods and immutability','10 min','String represents text and is immutable: operations create new strings rather than changing the original object. Use methods such as length, charAt, substring, equals and toUpperCase.', ['Use equals to compare string contents, not ==.','String indexes begin at zero.','Store the result of a string operation if you need to keep it.'], 'String course = "Java Programming";\nSystem.out.println(course.length());\nSystem.out.println(course.toUpperCase());\nSystem.out.println(course.substring(0, 4));\nSystem.out.println(course.equals("Java Programming"));', '16\nJAVA PROGRAMMING\nJava\ntrue', 'length returns the number of characters. toUpperCase returns an uppercase string. substring(0, 4) includes index 0 but excludes index 4, giving Java. equals compares character content and returns true for the same text.', 'Check whether a user-entered word equals "java" ignoring letter case.'),
L('07 · Arrays and strings','StringBuilder','7 min','Repeatedly joining strings inside a large loop can create many temporary String objects. StringBuilder provides a mutable sequence that is useful when constructing text piece by piece.', ['append adds content to the end.','toString produces a String snapshot.','StringBuilder is generally not synchronised for concurrent use.'], 'StringBuilder result = new StringBuilder();\nresult.append("Code");\nresult.append(". ");\nresult.append("Learn");\nSystem.out.println(result.toString());', 'Code. Learn', 'new StringBuilder creates a mutable text builder. Each append adds characters to the same builder. toString converts the accumulated content into a String for printing.', 'Build a comma-separated list of three favourite subjects.'),
L('08 · Object-oriented programming','Classes and objects','12 min','A class defines the data and behaviour that its objects have. An object is an instance created from the class. Fields hold state; methods describe behaviour.', ['A class is a blueprint, not an object itself.','new creates an object.','Use one responsibility per class where practical.'], 'class Student {\n    String name;\n    void introduce() {\n        System.out.println("I am " + name);\n    }\n}\n\nStudent student = new Student();\nstudent.name = "Asha";\nstudent.introduce();', 'I am Asha', 'class Student declares a new class. name is an instance field. introduce is an instance method that reads the field. new Student() constructs an object, the assignment stores Asha in its field, and student.introduce() calls the method on that object.', 'Create a Book class with title and a method that prints its title.'),
L('08 · Object-oriented programming','Constructors and this','10 min','A constructor initialises a new object. Its name matches the class name and it has no return type. this refers to the current object and helps distinguish fields from parameters.', ['A default constructor is provided only when you declare no constructor.','Constructor parameters let callers create valid objects.','this.field refers to the current object’s field.'], 'class Student {\n    String name;\n    int age;\n    Student(String name, int age) {\n        this.name = name;\n        this.age = age;\n    }\n}\nStudent s = new Student("Asha", 20);\nSystem.out.println(s.name + " " + s.age);', 'Asha 20', 'Student(String name, int age) is the constructor. this.name refers to the object field while name refers to the parameter. The assignments copy the provided values into the new object. The new expression calls the constructor with Asha and 20.', 'Add a constructor to a Product class that accepts name and price.'),
L('08 · Object-oriented programming','Encapsulation and access modifiers','10 min','Encapsulation protects an object’s internal state and exposes deliberate operations. Fields are commonly private, while public methods validate or return data. Java access modifiers control where members can be accessed.', ['private restricts access to the declaring class.','public makes a member accessible wherever the class is accessible.','Getters and setters should protect invariants, not just blindly expose fields.'], 'class BankAccount {\n    private double balance;\n    public void deposit(double amount) {\n        if (amount > 0) balance += amount;\n    }\n    public double getBalance() { return balance; }\n}\nBankAccount account = new BankAccount();\naccount.deposit(500);\nSystem.out.println(account.getBalance());', '500.0', 'balance is private, so callers cannot directly change it. deposit accepts only positive amounts before updating balance. getBalance returns the current value. The caller interacts through methods, which keeps validation in one place.', 'Add a withdraw method that rejects withdrawals greater than the balance.'),
L('08 · Object-oriented programming','Inheritance and super','10 min','Inheritance allows a subclass to extend an existing class. A subclass inherits accessible members and can add or override behaviour. super can call a superclass constructor or implementation.', ['Use inheritance for a genuine “is-a” relationship.','Java classes have single class inheritance.','Prefer composition when objects merely use another object.'], 'class Animal {\n    Animal(String name) { System.out.println(name); }\n    void sound() { System.out.println("Some sound"); }\n}\nclass Dog extends Animal {\n    Dog() { super("Dog"); }\n    @Override void sound() { System.out.println("Bark"); }\n}\nnew Dog().sound();', 'Dog\nBark', 'Dog extends Animal declares the subclass. Its constructor calls super("Dog") to invoke the parent constructor. @Override tells the compiler this method replaces the inherited sound implementation. new Dog() constructs the object and sound prints Bark.', 'Create a Vehicle parent class and a Car subclass with one extra field.'),
L('08 · Object-oriented programming','Polymorphism and overriding','10 min','Polymorphism lets a superclass reference point to a subclass object. When an overridable instance method is called, Java chooses the implementation based on the actual object at runtime.', ['A parent reference can point to a child instance.','Overriding changes inherited instance-method behaviour.','Runtime polymorphism makes code extensible.'], 'class Shape { void draw() { System.out.println("Shape"); } }\nclass Circle extends Shape {\n    @Override void draw() { System.out.println("Circle"); }\n}\nShape shape = new Circle();\nshape.draw();', 'Circle', 'shape is declared with the type Shape, but the actual object is Circle. The call shape.draw() dispatches to Circle’s overridden method at runtime, so Circle is printed.', 'Create Shape references for Circle and Rectangle and call draw on both.'),
L('08 · Object-oriented programming','Abstraction and abstract classes','9 min','Abstraction exposes what an object does while hiding details of how it does it. An abstract class can define shared code and abstract methods that concrete subclasses must implement.', ['An abstract class cannot be instantiated directly.','An abstract method has no body in the parent class.','Subclasses provide implementations unless they are abstract too.'], 'abstract class Payment {\n    abstract void pay(double amount);\n}\nclass CardPayment extends Payment {\n    @Override void pay(double amount) {\n        System.out.println("Paid " + amount + " by card");\n    }\n}\nPayment p = new CardPayment();\np.pay(250);', 'Paid 250.0 by card', 'Payment declares a contract: concrete payment types must implement pay. CardPayment supplies the method body. A Payment reference points to a CardPayment object, and calling pay invokes the concrete implementation.', 'Add a UpiPayment subclass with its own pay implementation.'),
L('08 · Object-oriented programming','Interfaces','10 min','An interface defines a contract that classes agree to implement. A class can implement multiple interfaces, which allows unrelated classes to share a capability without sharing a parent class.', ['Use implements to implement an interface.','A concrete class must implement its abstract interface methods.','Interfaces can also contain default and static methods.'], 'interface Printable {\n    void print();\n}\nclass Invoice implements Printable {\n    public void print() { System.out.println("Invoice printed"); }\n}\nPrintable item = new Invoice();\nitem.print();', 'Invoice printed', 'Printable declares the print contract. Invoice implements it and provides a public method body. The variable uses the interface type, which keeps the caller independent of the concrete class.', 'Create a Payable interface implemented by Invoice and Employee.'),
L('08 · Object-oriented programming','static members and final methods','8 min','static fields and methods belong to the class rather than each instance. final can prevent reassignment, method overriding or class inheritance depending on where it is applied.', ['Call static methods with ClassName.methodName when possible.','Static methods cannot directly access instance fields without an object.','final on a method prevents overriding; final on a class prevents subclassing.'], 'class Counter {\n    static int total = 0;\n    Counter() { total++; }\n}\nnew Counter();\nnew Counter();\nSystem.out.println(Counter.total);', '2', 'total is a static field shared by all Counter instances. Each constructor call increments that shared value. After two objects are created, Counter.total is 2. The class name is used to access the static field.', 'Add a static field that counts how many Student objects are created.'),
L('09 · Errors and exceptions','Exception handling with try/catch','10 min','Exceptions represent events that disrupt normal execution. Use try around operations that may fail and catch specific exception types when you can handle them meaningfully.', ['ArithmeticException can occur for integer division by zero.','Catch specific exceptions before broader ones.','Do not silently swallow an error without a reason.'], 'try {\n    int result = 10 / 0;\n    System.out.println(result);\n} catch (ArithmeticException ex) {\n    System.out.println("Cannot divide by zero");\n}', 'Cannot divide by zero', 'The division throws ArithmeticException, so the println inside try after the division is not reached. Java transfers control to the matching catch block, which prints a helpful message instead of letting this exception terminate the program.', 'Read two numbers and handle the case where the second is zero.'),
L('09 · Errors and exceptions','finally, throw and throws','9 min','finally is commonly used for cleanup that should happen whether the try block succeeds or fails. throw creates or rethrows an exception; throws declares exceptions a method may pass to its caller.', ['Use try-with-resources for AutoCloseable resources when possible.','throw is a statement; throws appears in a method signature.','Do not use exceptions as ordinary control flow.'], 'static void validateAge(int age) {\n    if (age < 18) {\n        throw new IllegalArgumentException("Age must be 18+");\n    }\n    System.out.println("Allowed");\n}', 'For age 16: IllegalArgumentException with message Age must be 18+', 'validateAge receives an integer. If age is less than 18, throw creates an IllegalArgumentException and exits the method immediately. Otherwise, execution reaches the Allowed print. A caller can catch the exception and respond appropriately.', 'Call validateAge with 16 and 20; handle the exception at the call site.'),
L('10 · Collections framework','ArrayList','9 min','ArrayList is a resizable list implementation. Unlike an array, it can grow and shrink. It stores object references, so primitive values use wrapper types such as Integer.', ['Use add to append and get(index) to read.','size() returns the number of elements.','Removing an element shifts later elements left.'], 'import java.util.ArrayList;\nArrayList<String> skills = new ArrayList<>();\nskills.add("Java");\nskills.add("SQL");\nskills.add("DSA");\nSystem.out.println(skills.get(0));\nSystem.out.println(skills.size());', 'Java\n3', 'The import makes ArrayList available. new ArrayList<String>() creates an empty list of strings. add appends each skill. get(0) reads the first element, while size returns the current count of three.', 'Remove SQL and print the remaining skills.'),
L('10 · Collections framework','HashSet and uniqueness','8 min','HashSet stores unique elements and is useful when you need fast membership checks on average. It does not guarantee iteration order.', ['add returns false when an equal element is already present.','Use contains to check membership.','Do not rely on HashSet display order.'], 'import java.util.HashSet;\nHashSet<Integer> values = new HashSet<>();\nvalues.add(10);\nvalues.add(20);\nvalues.add(10);\nSystem.out.println(values.size());\nSystem.out.println(values.contains(20));', '2\ntrue', 'HashSet<Integer> stores integer objects. Adding 10 twice does not create a second equal element. size therefore returns 2. contains(20) checks membership and returns true.', 'Use a HashSet to detect whether an array contains duplicates.'),
L('10 · Collections framework','HashMap and key-value pairs','10 min','HashMap maps keys to values. Keys are unique; putting another value under an existing key replaces the earlier mapping. HashMap does not guarantee iteration order.', ['Use put(key, value) to add or replace.','Use get(key) to retrieve; a missing key usually returns null.','Use getOrDefault to provide a fallback.'], 'import java.util.HashMap;\nHashMap<String, Integer> marks = new HashMap<>();\nmarks.put("Asha", 90);\nmarks.put("Ravi", 84);\nmarks.put("Asha", 95);\nSystem.out.println(marks.get("Asha"));\nSystem.out.println(marks.getOrDefault("Mia", 0));', '95\n0', 'The map stores student names as keys and marks as values. The second put for Asha replaces 90 with 95 because the key already exists. get returns 95. getOrDefault returns 0 because Mia is not present.', 'Count the frequency of each word in a sentence using HashMap.'),
L('10 · Collections framework','List, Set and Map choices','7 min','Choose a collection based on the behaviour you need. List preserves positional order and allows duplicates; Set enforces uniqueness; Map associates keys with values.', ['ArrayList is a common general-purpose List.','HashSet is useful for uniqueness and membership checks.','HashMap is useful for lookups by key.'], 'List<String> queue = new ArrayList<>();\nSet<String> unique = new HashSet<>();\nMap<String, Integer> score = new HashMap<>();\nqueue.add("A");\nunique.add("A");\nscore.put("A", 100);\nSystem.out.println(score.get("A"));', '100', 'The interface types List, Set and Map describe the behaviour expected from each variable. The concrete implementations are ArrayList, HashSet and HashMap. The last line retrieves the value mapped to key A.', 'For a shopping cart, choose a suitable collection for items, unique product IDs and quantities.'),
L('11 · Generics and enums','Generics basics','8 min','Generics allow classes and methods to work with specified reference types while preserving compile-time type safety. They reduce unsafe casts.', ['Use angle brackets to declare a type parameter.','Generics work with reference types; use Integer instead of int.','The compiler checks generic type compatibility.'], 'List<String> names = new ArrayList<>();\nnames.add("Asha");\nString first = names.get(0);\nSystem.out.println(first);', 'Asha', 'List<String> means this list is intended to contain String values. add accepts a String. get returns a String at compile time, so no manual cast is needed.', 'Create a List<Integer> and calculate its sum.'),
L('11 · Generics and enums','Enums','6 min','An enum represents a fixed set of named constants. It is safer and clearer than representing a small set of states with arbitrary strings or numbers.', ['Declare enum values in uppercase by convention.','Compare enum constants using ==.','Enums can have fields, constructors and methods.'], 'enum Status { NEW, IN_PROGRESS, DONE }\nStatus task = Status.IN_PROGRESS;\nif (task == Status.IN_PROGRESS) {\n    System.out.println("Work is active");\n}', 'Work is active', 'Status declares exactly three named values. task stores one of those enum constants. The equality check compares the selected enum value and prints the message only when it matches.', 'Create an OrderStatus enum with PLACED, SHIPPED and DELIVERED.'),
L('12 · File handling','Read and write a text file','10 min','Java provides APIs such as Files and Path for file operations. Use try-with-resources or methods that manage resources for you, and handle IOException where required.', ['Paths are represented by Path objects.','Files.writeString writes text; Files.readString reads it.','A file path must be valid and the program must have permission.'], 'import java.nio.file.Files;\nimport java.nio.file.Path;\nimport java.io.IOException;\n\nPath file = Path.of("notes.txt");\ntry {\n    Files.writeString(file, "Learn Java daily");\n    String text = Files.readString(file);\n    System.out.println(text);\n} catch (IOException ex) {\n    System.out.println("File operation failed");\n}', 'Learn Java daily', 'Path.of creates a path representing notes.txt. Files.writeString stores text at that path. Files.readString loads the text back. Both operations can throw IOException, so the catch block handles a file-related failure. The example uses a path relative to the working directory.', 'Write three lines to a file and read them back.'),
L('13 · Modern Java','Lambda expressions','8 min','A lambda is a concise way to provide an implementation of a functional interface. Lambdas are often used with collection operations and event handlers.', ['A functional interface has one abstract method.','The arrow separates parameters from the body.','Keep lambda bodies short and readable.'], 'List<String> names = List.of("Ravi", "Asha", "Mia");\nnames.forEach(name -> System.out.println(name));', 'Ravi\nAsha\nMia', 'List.of creates an unmodifiable list. forEach calls the supplied action for each item. The lambda parameter name receives one string at a time, and the body prints it.', 'Use a lambda to print each number doubled.'),
L('13 · Modern Java','Streams: filter and map','10 min','Streams describe a sequence of operations on data. Intermediate operations such as filter and map are lazy; a terminal operation such as toList or forEach triggers processing.', ['filter keeps elements matching a predicate.','map transforms each element.','A stream pipeline does not usually mutate the original collection.'], 'List<Integer> nums = List.of(1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6);\nList<Integer> squares = nums.stream()\n    .filter(n -> n % 2 == 0)\n    .map(n -> n * n)\n    .toList();\nSystem.out.println(squares);', '[4, 16, 36]', 'stream starts a pipeline. filter keeps only even values because n % 2 == 0. map transforms each remaining value into its square. toList collects the result into a list. This toList API is available in modern Java versions.', 'Filter numbers greater than 10 and convert them to strings.'),
L('14 · Multithreading','Threads and Runnable','10 min','A thread is an independent path of execution. Implementing Runnable separates the task from the thread that runs it. Scheduling order is not guaranteed unless coordination is used.', ['Call start() to ask the JVM to run a new thread.','Calling run() directly is just an ordinary method call.','Shared mutable data can cause race conditions.'], 'Runnable task = () -> System.out.println("Task running");\nThread worker = new Thread(task);\nworker.start();\nSystem.out.println("Main continues");', 'Task running and Main continues; order may vary.', 'The lambda implements Runnable.run. new Thread(task) creates a thread object. start asks the JVM to schedule execution on a new thread. The main thread continues independently, so either message may appear first.', 'Run two worker threads and observe why output order may vary.'),
L('14 · Multithreading','synchronized and race conditions','10 min','A race condition occurs when correctness depends on the timing of threads accessing shared state. synchronized ensures only one thread at a time enters a synchronized region guarded by the same monitor.', ['Protect shared mutable state with a consistent locking strategy.','volatile provides visibility for some cases but does not make compound operations atomic.','Prefer higher-level concurrency utilities when appropriate.'], 'class Counter {\n    private int count = 0;\n    synchronized void increment() { count++; }\n    synchronized int getCount() { return count; }\n}', 'This class provides synchronized increments and reads.', 'count++ is a read-modify-write operation, not one indivisible machine action. synchronized locks the current Counter instance while each method executes, preventing two threads from incrementing this same counter simultaneously. The getter uses the same lock to coordinate access.', 'Create two threads that each increment a shared counter 1,000 times and verify the final count.'),
L('15 · JVM and memory','Stack, heap and garbage collection','8 min','The JVM manages runtime memory. Method calls use stack frames for local variables and call state; objects generally live on the heap. Garbage collection reclaims heap objects that are no longer reachable.', ['Local variables and object references are different concepts.','A reference can point to an object stored on the heap.','Garbage collection timing is managed by the JVM, not guaranteed at a specific moment.'], 'Student s = new Student("Asha", 20);\nStudent another = s;\nanother = null;\nSystem.out.println(s.name);', 'Asha', 'new Student creates an object. s stores a reference to that object. another copies the same reference, so both variables initially refer to one object. Setting another to null clears only that variable; s still refers to the object, so its name can be printed.', 'Draw a simple reference diagram for two variables pointing to one object.'),
L('16 · Build skills','Packages and imports','7 min','Packages organise related classes and help avoid name collisions. The package declaration, when present, appears at the beginning of the source file. import lets you refer to types without writing their full package name each time.', ['Package names are commonly lowercase.','Folder structure usually mirrors package names.','java.lang types such as String are imported automatically.'], 'package academy.basics;\n\nimport java.util.ArrayList;\n\npublic class CourseList {\n    ArrayList<String> courses = new ArrayList<>();\n}', 'No output; this declares a class in academy.basics.', 'The package line places CourseList in academy.basics. The import makes ArrayList available by its short name. The field creates an ArrayList intended to contain course names. In a real project, the file should be located under academy/basics relative to the source root.', 'Create a package called academy.models and place a Student class inside it.'),
L('16 · Build skills','equals, hashCode and toString','9 min','Object provides methods that many classes override. equals defines logical equality, hashCode supports hash-based collections, and toString provides a readable text representation.', ['If two objects are equal according to equals, they must have equal hashCode values.','Do not use == to compare distinct objects by content.','Modern Java records can generate these methods for simple data carriers.'], 'class Student {\n    String name;\n    Student(String name) { this.name = name; }\n    @Override public String toString() { return "Student(" + name + ")"; }\n}\nSystem.out.println(new Student("Asha"));', 'Student(Asha)', 'The constructor stores the name. toString overrides Object.toString with a more useful representation. When println receives the object, it uses that string representation. For value-based equality, also implement equals and hashCode consistently or consider a record.', 'Override toString in a Product class and print a Product object.'),
L('17 · Testing and debugging','Read compiler errors and debug','8 min','A compiler error means source code could not be compiled. A runtime exception happens while the program runs. A logic bug means the program runs but gives an incorrect result. Debug by narrowing the problem and checking assumptions.', ['Read the first relevant error and its line number.','Check braces, spelling, types and semicolons.','Use a debugger or temporary print statements to inspect values.'], 'int total = 0;\nfor (int i = 1; i <= 3; i++) {\n    total += i;\n}\nSystem.out.println(total);', '6', 'total begins at zero. The loop adds 1, then 2, then 3, producing 6. If the result is unexpected, inspect total and i at each iteration to find where the state first differs from your expectation.', 'Intentionally change <= to < and explain why the output changes.'),
L('18 · Practice projects','Mini project: student grade calculator','20 min','Combine input, variables, decisions and methods into a small program. Validate that marks are within a sensible range before calculating a grade.', ['Break input, validation and grade selection into separate steps.','Use descriptive names.','Test boundary values, not just one typical input.'], 'static String gradeFor(int marks) {\n    if (marks < 0 || marks > 100) return "Invalid marks";\n    if (marks >= 90) return "A";\n    if (marks >= 75) return "B";\n    if (marks >= 60) return "C";\n    return "Needs improvement";\n}\nSystem.out.println(gradeFor(82));', 'B', 'The method first rejects values outside 0–100. It then checks grade thresholds from highest to lowest so the first matching branch wins. 82 is below 90 but at least 75, so the method returns B. The caller prints the returned string.', 'Add Scanner input and test 101, -1, 90, 75, 60 and 59.'),
L('18 · Practice projects','Mini project: bank account','25 min','A small bank account project reinforces classes, encapsulation, constructors, validation and methods. In a real financial system, money handling needs careful decimal and audit design; this example is for learning only.', ['Keep balance private.','Reject zero or negative deposits and invalid withdrawals.','Return status or throw a clear exception when an operation fails.'], 'class BankAccount {\n    private double balance;\n    BankAccount(double openingBalance) {\n        if (openingBalance < 0) throw new IllegalArgumentException();\n        balance = openingBalance;\n    }\n    void deposit(double amount) { if (amount > 0) balance += amount; }\n    boolean withdraw(double amount) {\n        if (amount <= 0 || amount > balance) return false;\n        balance -= amount; return true;\n    }\n    double getBalance() { return balance; }\n}', 'Create an account, deposit money, withdraw an allowed amount, and print the remaining balance.', 'The constructor validates the opening balance before storing it. deposit changes the private field only for positive amounts. withdraw rejects non-positive amounts and amounts larger than the balance; otherwise it subtracts the amount and returns true. getBalance exposes the current balance without allowing direct assignment. Production financial code should use BigDecimal and robust rules.', 'Add a transfer method and test insufficient funds.'),
L('19 · Interview readiness','Common Java interview concepts','12 min','Interview preparation is strongest when you can explain a concept, write a small example, and describe trade-offs. These questions connect fundamentals to everyday development.', ['JDK develops, JVM executes bytecode; JRE is a runtime concept.','Overloading is resolved from parameter lists; overriding enables runtime polymorphism.','ArrayList is indexed and allows duplicates; HashSet enforces uniqueness; HashMap maps keys to values.'], 'String a = new String("java");\nString b = new String("java");\nSystem.out.println(a == b);\nSystem.out.println(a.equals(b));', 'false\ntrue', 'Each new String expression creates a distinct String object, so == compares different references and returns false. equals compares the character content, which is the same, so it returns true. This distinction is a classic Java interview topic.', 'Explain == versus equals, checked versus unchecked exceptions, and interface versus abstract class aloud.'),
L('19 · Interview readiness','Final revision and capstone checklist','15 min','Before calling the foundations course complete, build one small application from a blank file without copying the whole solution. Explain your design and test invalid inputs as well as normal inputs.', ['Know syntax, types, decisions, loops, methods, arrays and strings.','Practise OOP, exceptions, collections, files and basic streams.','Use Git to save your project and write a README with run instructions.'], 'public class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        System.out.println("Build something useful.");\n    }\n}', 'Build something useful.', 'This final program revisits the class declaration, main entry point and output statement. The larger goal is not to memorise this snippet: use the course concepts to design, implement, test and explain a complete small project independently.', 'Capstone: build a console-based Student Management System with add, list, search and grade features.')
];

export default function JavaCourse() {
  const [active, setActive] = useState(() => { try { return Number(localStorage.getItem('2409-java-active') || 0); } catch { return 0; } });
  const [done, setDone] = useState(() => { try { return JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem('2409-java-lessons-done') || '[]'); } catch { return []; } });
  const [notes, setNotes] = useState(() => { try { return localStorage.getItem('2409-java-notes') || ''; } catch { return ''; } });
  const [query, setQuery] = useState('');
  const lesson = lessons[active] || lessons[0];
  const sections = useMemo(() => [...new Set(lessons.map(x => x.section))], []);
  const filtered = useMemo(() => lessons.map((item, index) => ({ ...item, index })).filter(item => `${item.title} ${item.section}`.toLowerCase().includes(query.toLowerCase())), [query]);
  const percent = Math.round(done.length / lessons.length * 100);
  const choose = i => { setActive(i); try { localStorage.setItem('2409-java-active', String(i)); } catch {} };
  const markDone = () => setDone(old => { const next = old.includes(active) ? old.filter(n => n !== active) : [...old, active]; try { localStorage.setItem('2409-java-lessons-done', JSON.stringify(next)); } catch {} return next; });
  const copyCode = async () => { try { await navigator.clipboard.writeText(lesson.code); } catch {} };
  return <div className="java-page">
    <div className="java-topline">2409 ACADEMY <span>COMPLETE JAVA COURSE · BEGINNER TO PRACTICAL</span></div>
    <header className="java-nav"><a className="java-brand" href="/"><img src={logo} alt="2409 Academy"/><span><b>2409 <em>ACADEMY</em></b><small>CODE · LEARN · BUILD · LEAD</small></span></a><a className="java-back" href="/explore"><ArrowLeft size={16}/> Explore all paths</a></header>
    <main className="java-layout">
      <aside className="java-sidebar"><div className="java-label"><BookOpen size={15}/> JAVA MASTERCLASS</div><h2>From first line to <em>real projects.</em></h2><p>A structured Java course with examples, expected output, line-by-line walkthroughs and a practice task in every lesson.</p>
        <div className="java-progress"><div><span>Course progress</span><b>{percent}%</b></div><i><span style={{width:`${percent}%`}}/></i><small>{done.length} of {lessons.length} lessons complete</small></div>
        <label className="java-search"><Search size={15}/><input value={query} onChange={e=>setQuery(e.target.value)} placeholder="Find a Java topic…"/></label>
        <div className="java-lesson-list">{sections.map(section => { const group = filtered.filter(x=>x.section===section); if (!group.length) return null; return <div className="java-module" key={section}><small>{section}</small>{group.map(item=><button key={item.index} className={active===item.index?'active':''} onClick={()=>choose(item.index)}><span className="java-lesson-check">{done.includes(item.index)?<CheckCircle2 size={17}/>:String(item.index+1).padStart(2,'0')}</span><span><b>{item.title}</b><small>{item.time}</small></span></button>)}</div>; })}</div>
        <div className="java-sidebar-note"><GraduationCap size={18}/> <span>Learn at your pace. Progress and notes are saved in this browser.</span></div>
      </aside>
      <article className="java-lesson"><div className="java-kicker"><span/> {lesson.section.toUpperCase()} · LESSON {String(active+1).padStart(2,'0')} / {String(lessons.length).padStart(2,'0')}</div><h1>{lesson.title}<em>.</em></h1><p className="java-intro">{lesson.content}</p>
        <h3>Key takeaways</h3><ul className="java-points">{lesson.points.map(point=><li key={point}><CheckCircle2 size={17}/><span>{point}</span></li>)}</ul>
        <div className="java-code-card"><div className="java-code-head"><span><Terminal size={16}/> Code example</span><button onClick={copyCode}><Copy size={14}/> Copy code</button></div><pre><code>{lesson.code}</code></pre><div className="java-output"><b>EXPECTED OUTPUT / RESULT</b><pre>{lesson.output}</pre></div></div>
        <section className="java-walkthrough"><div className="java-walkthrough-title"><ListChecks size={18}/><h3>Understand it line by line</h3></div><p>{lesson.walkthrough}</p></section>
        <div className="java-note-box"><b>Practice challenge</b><p>{lesson.challenge}</p></div>
        <label className="java-notes"><span>Your lesson notes</span><textarea value={notes} onChange={e=>{setNotes(e.target.value);try{localStorage.setItem('2409-java-notes',e.target.value)}catch{}}} placeholder="Write what you understood, questions, and mistakes to revisit…"/></label>
        <div className="java-lesson-actions"><button className={done.includes(active)?'java-completed':'java-primary'} onClick={markDone}>{done.includes(active)?'✓ Lesson completed — undo':'Mark lesson complete'}</button><button className="java-next" onClick={()=>choose((active+1)%lessons.length)}>{active===lessons.length-1?'Back to first lesson':'Next lesson'} <ArrowRight size={16}/></button></div>
        <div className="java-course-bottom"><b>Study tip</b><p>Read the explanation, predict the output before looking, type the code yourself, then change one thing and run it again. Understanding grows when you experiment.</p><button onClick={()=>{if(window.confirm('Reset all Java lesson completion marks on this browser?')){setDone([]);try{localStorage.setItem('2409-java-lessons-done','[]')}catch{}}}}><RotateCcw size={14}/> Reset course progress</button></div>
        <p className="java-disclaimer">2409 Academy educational content. Examples are designed for learning; run them locally and test edge cases. Your notes and progress are stored on this device only.</p>
      </article>
    </main>
    <footer className="java-footer">© {new Date().getFullYear()} 2409 Academy <span>THE HARDEST CHOICES REQUIRE THE STRONGEST WILL.</span></footer>
  </div>;
}
